      "text": "To find the value of \\(\\frac{a \\cdot c}{b \\cdot d}\\), we need to express \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) in terms of \\(d\\). Let's start with the given relationships:\n\n1. \\(a\\) is three times \\(b\\):\n   \\[\n   a = 3b\n   \\]\n\n2. \\(b\\) is twice \\(c\\):\n   \\[\n   b = 2c\n   \\]\n\n3. \\(c\\) is four times \\(d\\):\n   \\[\n   c = 4d\n   \\]\n\nNow, we need to express \\(a\\) in terms of \\(d\\). Using the relationship \\(c = 4d\\), we substitute \\(c\\) in the expression for \\(b\\):\n   \\[\n   b = 2c = 2(4d) = 8d\n   \\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\(b = 8d\\) into the expression for \\(a\\):\n   \\[\n   a = 3b = 3(8d) = 24d\n   \\]\n\nNow we have \\(a = 24d\\) and \\(b = 8d\\). We substitute these into the expression \\(\\frac{a \\cdot c}{b \\cdot d}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{a \\cdot c}{b \\cdot d} = \\frac{(24d) \\cdot (4d)}{(8d) \\cdot d}\n   \\]\n\nSimplify the expression:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{24d \\cdot 4d}{8d \\cdot d} = \\frac{96d^2}{8d^2}\n   \\]\n\nThe \\(d^2\\) terms cancel out:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{96d^2}{8d^2} = \\frac{96}{8} = 12\n   \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\frac{a \\cdot c}{b \\cdot d}\\) is \\(\\boxed{12}\\).",
